# AGENTS.md — Multi-Agent Orchestration Specification for `{{MODULE_NAME}}`

This is the **normative** specification for every AI agent that works on this
Puppet module (Claude Code, Codex/GPT, Copilot, Cursor, Aider, …). Human
contributors follow the same rules.

Companion documents, all subordinate to this one:

| File | Scope |
| --- | --- |
| [CLAUDE.md](CLAUDE.md) | Claude-specific behaviour (CTO / architect / reviewer) |
| [skills/claude/SKILLS.md](skills/claude/SKILLS.md) | Claude strategic-orchestrator routing rules |
| [skills/codex/SKILLS.md](skills/codex/SKILLS.md) | Codex engineering-orchestrator routing rules |

When two documents disagree, **this file wins**. The numbers in §5–§9 are the
single source of truth and are repeated verbatim in the skill files.

---

## 0. Project invariants (non-negotiable, override all routing)

`{{MODULE_NAME}}` is a Puppet module. The canonical interface is the manifests in
`manifests/`, with supporting Ruby under `lib/`, templates in `templates/`, and
tests in `spec/`.

**[Regent](https://github.com/felipe-quintella/regent) is the single tool for
validating and testing this module.** Regent ships a self-contained binary with
an embedded Ruby runner, so no host Ruby toolchain is required — and none may be
introduced.

- Never invoke `puppet`, `bundle exec`, `rspec`, `rake`, `gem`, or `pdk` directly.
- A missing gem is fixed with `regent bootstrap`, never `gem install`.
- Never edit `pkg/` by hand; it is build output.

```sh
regent validate    # parse manifests + metadata.json, lint
regent test        # run rspec-puppet specs through the embedded runner
regent build       # produce a Forge-ready tarball in pkg/
```

Any plan that reintroduces a host Ruby or PDK dependency is rejected regardless
of which agent produced it.

