## 3. Model registry — two families only

Every model is either **Claude family** or **GPT family**. No third-party model
is part of this architecture.

**GPT family — the Codex Engineering Orchestrator's models**

| Model | Class | Use for | Relative cost |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| **GPT-6 Mini** | fast coder | Default implementation, manifests, specs, refactors | 1× |
| **GPT-6** | deep coder/architect | Complex architecture, multi-class design, hard debugging | 8× |
| **GPT Terra** | long-context reader | Large control repos, bulk comprehension, map phase of map/reduce | 2× |
| **GPT Sol** | simulation | Rollout forecasting, blast-radius and cost modelling | 6× |

**Claude family — the Strategic Orchestrator's models**

| Model | Class | Use for | Relative cost |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| **Claude Sonnet** | primary reviewer/planner | Code review, planning, synthesis | 3× |
| **Claude Opus** | escalation reviewer | Architecture review, conflict resolution, risk analysis | 12× |

### Family discipline (normative)

1. **One family per interaction.** When Claude is the acting agent it runs on a
   Claude model. When Codex is the acting agent it runs on a GPT model. An agent
   never switches family mid-task.
2. **Families meet only at an orchestrator handoff.** Codex hands a finished diff
   to Claude for review; Claude hands a Task Packet to Codex for implementation.
   Those two boundaries are the only cross-family traffic.
3. **Claude Sonnet is the Claude-family default; Claude Opus is escalation only.**
   Sonnet costs a quarter of Opus, so routing routine review to Sonnet is the
   single largest token saving available on the Claude side.
4. **GPT-6 Mini is the GPT-family default.** GPT-6, Terra, and Sol are reached
   only by the routing rules in §4.

Cost multipliers are planning weights, not billing figures. Use them to compare
routes, not to predict invoices.

---

## 4. Model routing policy (deterministic)

Evaluate in order. The first rule whose condition holds wins; stop evaluating.

| # | Condition | Primary | Reviewer | Escalation |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| R1 | Enterprise planning: ≥3 teams **or** horizon ≥2 quarters **or** org-wide policy | **GPT-6 + Claude Opus + GPT Sol** (Opus synthesises) | Human | Human |
| R2 | Architecture review: the artifact is a design doc, ADR, or parameter/API contract | **Claude Opus** | — | GPT-6 second opinion |
| R3 | Simulation or forecasting (rollout, blast radius, cost, capacity) | **GPT Sol** | Claude Sonnet | Claude Opus |
| R4 | Large repository: >200 files in scope **or** >150 000 tokens of context after compression | **GPT Terra** (map/reduce to a ≤4 000-token brief) | Claude Sonnet | Re-enter at R1 with the brief |
| R5 | Complex architecture: new subsystem, ≥3 classes/modules touched, **or** a public parameter changes | **GPT-6** | Claude Opus | Human |
| R6 | Coding: manifests, functions, specs, debugging, refactor within an existing pattern | **GPT-6 Mini** | **Claude Sonnet** | GPT-6 |
| R7 | Simple task: single file, <50 LOC delta, no parameter change, no security surface | **GPT-6 Mini** | — | GPT-6 Mini + Claude Sonnet |

**Notes**

- R4 never produces the final answer; it produces a compressed brief which is
  then routed from R1 again. It is the only rule that may re-enter the table.
- A Security Engineer finding of severity High or Critical forces the task up one
  escalation tier regardless of which rule matched.
- Claude is **never** the default code generator.

